<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>Getting the Most from AI · Atil Ilerialkan</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Reference capability deck — every slide kind this web-presentation system can build, exercised once."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <!-- Persistent mono "instrument" chrome + keyboard legend + chapter wash.
         Populated / driven by the controller below. -->
    <div class="deck-chrome" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="chrome-section">
        <b>&#9632;</b> <span data-chrome-section>Getting more from AI</span>
      </div>
      <div class="chrome-progress">
        <span class="now" data-chrome-now>01</span> / <span data-chrome-total>00</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="deck-legend">
      <kbd>&larr;</kbd><kbd>&rarr;</kbd> move ·
      <kbd>&darr;</kbd> sub-slide ·
      <kbd>ESC</kbd> overview ·
      <kbd>s</kbd> notes ·
      <kbd>p</kbd> presenter ·
      <kbd>f</kbd> full ·
      <kbd>q</kbd> quit
    </div>
    <div class="chapter-wash" data-wash aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <!-- Inline presenter-notes overlay (native-proto style), toggled by "s".
         Shows the current slide's <aside class="notes"> text without leaving
         the deck. reveal's separate presenter window moves to "p". -->
    <aside class="deck-notes" data-notes hidden>
      <div class="deck-notes__head">
        <span>Presenter notes</span>
        <span><kbd>S</kbd> close · <kbd>P</kbd> presenter view</span>
      </div>
      <div class="deck-notes__body" data-notes-body>
        Notes for the current slide appear here.
      </div>
    </aside>

    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="slides">
        <!-- 1 ───────────────────────────── HERO (eyebrow + big headline) -->
        <section class="hero" data-section="Opening">
          <!-- Eyebrow, title and sub-line each do a DIFFERENT job (Atil,
               2026-08-02): the old eyebrow "Getting the Most from AI" was a
               paraphrase of the old title "GETTING MORE FROM AI." and said
               nothing twice. Now: the eyebrow is the provocation, the title
               the promise, the sub-line the contents.

               "Coding agents" is deliberately vendor-neutral — the company
               runs Copilot, but nothing in this deck depends on which
               provider, and no slide names one. Chapter 2 goes on to take the
               word apart (model + harness + skills + tools), so the title
               poses a noun the deck later defines rather than assuming it. -->
          <span class="eyebrow">Same licence. Different results.</span>
          <!-- Noun first, so the accent lands on the qualifier rather than the
               subject. Two hand-broken lines: the previous phrasing needed
               three at hero scale, which crowded the block. -->
          <h1 class="h-set">
            <span class="h-line"><span>CODING AGENTS,</span></span>
            <span class="h-line"><span class="accent">AT THEIR BEST.</span></span>
          </h1>
          <!-- Atil's own wording. Four beats, and they are the four factors in
               order — how it works (ch2), what it costs (ch3), less waste (ch4,
               "the principles that cut waste"), consistent (ch5, "the process
               that repeats"). The roadmap slide is this line drawn. It must
               stay on ONE line; don't pad it back into a sentence. -->
          <p class="hero-sub">How it works, what it costs, less waste, consistent.</p>
          <p class="byline">Atil Ilerialkan · live from atilileri.github.io</p>
          <aside class="notes">
            Ninety seconds, standing still. You are already paying for AI, and
            the bill is going up — so this is not a talk about whether to use
            it. It's about getting more back from what you are already buying.

            Lean on the eyebrow: everyone in this room has the same licence.
            The results are not the same. That gap is the whole talk, and it
            is not a procurement problem.

            Nothing here depends on which provider — we happen to run Copilot,
            but no slide names one, and everything shown works the same way
            whatever is behind it. Say that once, here, so nobody spends the
            next forty minutes wondering.

            Two promises up front: nothing here is a demo of a product you
            can't have, and every number on screen is one we can source. Then
            straight into the objection on the next slide, in their own words.
            Press S any time for these inline notes; P opens the separate
            presenter window.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             CHAPTER 01 — "Can't we just throw money at it?" (wayfinder #29).
             Five slides: the objection as a wash divider → the DeepSWE bench
             chart → the determinism slide → the four-factor roadmap → the
             lottery, moved here as a PROMISE rather than the pay-off it used
             to be at the end of the deck.

             This is one of only TWO full `.chapter` washes left in the deck
             (chapter 1 and chapter 6). Chapters 2–5 get the roadmap divider
             instead, so the wash keeps its impact — map #29, resolving #20's
             fog about the wash's own "2–3× in the real talk" ceiling.

             All the copy in this chapter is LOCKED — the wash and the roadmap
             by #34, the stop-point slide by #33, the lottery's headline by
             #34. The wash carries the objection bare, with no concession
             sub-line: printing the concession would fire #33's "You can't buy
             your way to spec." two slides early, on furniture. The chapter
             runs objection → ceiling → the turn → the route → the promise.
             ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

        <!-- 1a ─────────────────── CHAPTER divider (triggers chapter-wash) -->
        <section class="chapter" data-section="01 — Why optimize" data-chapter>
          <span class="chapter-no">CHAPTER 01</span>
          <h2>Can&rsquo;t we just throw<br />money at problems?</h2>
          <!-- Atil, 2026-08-02: this REVERSES #34, which rejected a wash
               sub-line on the grounds that a concession would fire #33's
               "You can't buy your way to spec." two slides early. The line
               below avoids that trap by staying a QUESTION in the room's own
               voice — it concedes the models are good without conceding what
               that buys, so slide 3 still lands its answer unspent. Both
               lines are theirs; the deck answers neither until slide 3. -->
          <p class="chapter-sub">The models are good now. <br />Isn&rsquo;t that enough?</p>
          <aside class="notes">
            Say both lines in their voice, not yours — this is the objection
            the room is already holding, and naming it out loud first is what
            buys you the next forty minutes.

            The second line is the real one. Concede it hard and mean it: the
            models ARE good now, better than last year, and for a lot of work
            they are genuinely enough. Don't argue with it here — you have no
            evidence on screen yet and you'd just sound defensive.

            What you're setting up is the missing half: whether a good model
            on its own gets you there, or whether it still takes someone who
            knows how to work it. That's the question this whole talk answers,
            and the next three slides answer it with data rather than opinion.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 5b ──────────────────── INTERACTIVE BENCHMARK CHART (DeepSWE v1.1) -->
        <section class="bench" data-section="01 — Why optimize" data-bench>
          <span class="eyebrow">Interactive · hover a model, click a family</span>
          <h2 class="h-set">
            <span class="h-line"><span>DeepSWE v1.1 &mdash; capability vs. cost</span></span>
          </h2>
          <div class="bench-bar">
            <div class="bench-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Chart view">
              <button type="button" data-view="scatter" class="on">Score vs. cost</button>
              <button type="button" data-view="bars">Ranked pass rate</button>
            </div>
            <div class="bench-legend" data-bench-legend></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bench-plot">
            <svg data-bench-svg viewBox="0 0 1000 570" role="img"
              aria-label="DeepSWE v1.1 results: pass rate against average cost per task, one curve per model across reasoning-effort levels."
            ></svg>
            <div class="bench-tip" data-bench-tip hidden></div>
          </div>
          <p class="bench-note">
            113 tasks · one line per model across reasoning-effort levels · source:
            <span class="mono">deepswe.datacurve.ai/blog/deepswe-v1-1</span><br />
            The best score anywhere on the board is 72.7% &mdash; no model, at any
            effort, at any price, clears it.
          </p>
          <aside class="notes">
            The live leaderboard, rebuilt inside the deck — same data, our palette.
            Hover any point for cost, output tokens and steps; click a family chip to
            isolate it; the toggle swaps the scatter for the ranked bars with error
            bars.

            This chart proves two things, and this slide only carries one of
            them. First — the one you make here — THERE IS A CEILING: 72.7% is
            the best anything does, so even spending everything you are not at
            100%. That means there is still room to improve, still a human in
            the loop, and still a process to engineer so you reach that ceiling
            as often, as consistently and by as short a path as possible.
            Second — marginal effort stops paying, so WHERE YOU STOP is the
            decision — belongs to the next slide. Don't spend it here; the next
            slide walks one of these curves and turns it over.

            One piece of context worth saying aloud, flagged as context: today's
            models turn sloppier questions into better outputs than any prior
            generation, so you genuinely can throw money at more problems than
            you could a year ago. THE CHART DOES NOT PROVE THAT — it is a
            comparison across years and this is a single snapshot. Say it as
            your own observation, not as something on screen.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 1c ─── THE STOP-POINT: money → method (#33, built by #37).
             Replaces the old "we are engineers" slide and is the chapter's
             argumentative hinge. Deliberately NOT a new widget: it is the
             previous slide reduced to one curve, one marker and one number,
             so it reads as a callback rather than a fourth demand on the
             room's attention.

             The chart's two proofs are split one per slide — the bench keeps
             THE CEILING, this slide takes THE STOP-POINT — so neither slide
             argues the other's point. The word "determinism" never appears on
             screen; the room's own phrase is "the repeatable, optimum way to
             spec". Forwards, the negative headline makes the roadmap arrive
             as the answer to a question money just failed. -->
        <section class="stop" data-section="01 — Why optimize" data-stop>
          <span class="eyebrow">The turn &middot; press &rarr; to spend more</span>
          <h2 class="h-set">
            <span class="h-line"><span>You can&rsquo;t buy your way to spec.</span></span>
          </h2>
          <p class="tk-body">
            Past a point, more effort buys nothing &mdash; and no amount buys the
            same answer twice.
          </p>
          <div class="stop-plot">
            <svg data-stop-svg viewBox="0 0 900 468" role="img"
              aria-label="One model's spend curve from the previous chart. Each step of reasoning effort costs more per task; the score climbs steeply at first, then flattens, and the last and most expensive step scores slightly lower than the one before it."
            ></svg>
            <p class="stop-read" data-stop-read aria-live="polite"></p>
          </div>
          <!-- Atil, 2026-08-02: "A better way is …", not #33's "What you want
               is …", and promoted OUT of the small-type caveat slot. It is the
               line the whole slide exists to deliver, and at caveat size it
               was reading as a footnote to the chart rather than the answer to
               the headline. -->
          <p class="stop-turn" data-stop-turn>
            A better way is the <b>repeatable</b>, <b>optimum way</b> to spec.
          </p>
          <!-- Four walk fragments (one per step along the curve) + a fifth that
               greys the curve and lands the turn. Nothing here is counted: the
               step is DERIVED from how many fragments reveal has made visible,
               which is what makes → and ← replay identically and re-entry from
               either direction land on the right frame (#26's rule). -->
          <span class="fragment stop-step" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fragment stop-step" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fragment stop-step" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fragment stop-step" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="fragment stop-step" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <aside class="notes">
            This is the pivot of the whole talk: from money to method. Same
            data as the slide before, one model lifted out of it — so you are
            not asking the room to learn anything new, you are asking them to
            look again.

            Walk it with →. Each press is one step up in reasoning effort, and
            the number beside the marker is the MARGINAL spend — what that step
            cost you on top of the last one, not the running total. First step:
            about €2 more buys nearly six points. Good deal. Keep going and
            each step costs more and buys less. The last step spends 61% more
            than the one before it — €7.62 more per task — and scores 0.2
            points LOWER. Let the line turn over on screen before you say
            anything; the dip is real data, not a diagram.

            Then land the phrase: what you actually want is the repeatable,
            optimum way to spec. Three words doing three jobs — REPEATABLE, the
            same way twice; OPTIMUM, where you stop spending; TO SPEC, your
            target, not the top of the board.

            This is a REFRAME, not an argument against spending. "Optimum" is a
            spending instruction. The room has just approved a budget; don't
            tell them not to spend it, tell them where it stops working.

            The headline is a question money cannot answer, and the next slide
            answers it — "Four things decide what you get back." Hand straight
            over.

            Guard against the lottery two slides from now: this slide STATES
            repeatability, the lottery SHOWS the spread. This one is about the
            lever; that one is about the result.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 1d ─────────────────── THE ROADMAP (all four factors, equal) -->
        <RoadmapDivider
          section="01 — Why optimize"
          heading="Four things decide what you get back."
        >
          <aside class="notes">
            This answers the question the slide before just asked. Money
            couldn't buy you spec; these four can — so arrive on this slide as
            a reply, not as a new topic.

            The map for the rest of the talk. You will see these same four, in
            these same positions, every time we enter a chapter — one of them
            lit. Never explain that device; just walk it, and by the third
            chapter the room is reading it without being told.

            The gloss lines under each label appear ONLY here — the dividers
            stay bare so their later appearances read as recognition. This is
            also the one and only time "Tokenomics" gets defined on screen, so
            let it land: what it costs. Don't sell any single factor here — the
            promise on the next slide hangs off all four together, and its
            headline finishes this one's sentence.
          </aside>
        </RoadmapDivider>

        <!-- 8b ─────────────────── RELIABILITY SCATTER (result quality × spend) -->
        <section class="lottery" data-section="01 — Why optimize" data-lottery>
          <span class="eyebrow">The promise &middot; press &rarr; to level up</span>
          <!-- The headline finishes the roadmap's own sentence — "Four things
               decide what you get back." → "This is what you get back." — so
               the two slides fuse into one beat and the promise is attributed
               to all four factors without naming them again (#34). The old
               headline is kept, demoted into the deck's standing one-sentence
               slot, which this slide used to skip. It stays up through BOTH
               states of the collapse: that is what makes it read as a promise
               rather than a caption on the scatter. -->
          <h2 class="h-set">
            <span class="h-line"><span>This is what you get back.</span></span>
          </h2>
          <p class="tk-body">Your results don&rsquo;t have to be a lottery.</p>
          <div class="lottery-plot">
            <svg data-lottery-svg viewBox="0 0 980 600" role="img"
              aria-label="Result quality against tokens spent. A regular user's outputs are scattered across the whole field — some ship, most miss, several overshoot the budget. After the training, the same attempts converge into a tight cluster in the ship-ready, on-budget corner: consistent quality at a consistent, minimal spend."
            ></svg>
          </div>
          <!-- The caption block (the on-target stat and the before/after
               sub-copy) was REMOVED at Atil's call, 2026-08-02. The scatter
               makes the argument on its own, and the lower-left text was
               competing with it. Both numbers and both readings now live in
               the presenter note — the deck's standing rule that notes carry
               what the slide can't. `setLottery` still drives the collapse;
               it just has no text left to update. -->
          <span class="fragment lottery-trigger" data-lottery-collapse aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <aside class="notes">
            The promise of the whole talk, made up front — this is chapter 1's
            last slide, not the deck's pay-off, so state it as something you
            are going to earn over the next four chapters rather than something
            just demonstrated. The headline finishes the sentence the roadmap
            started, so run the two together: four things decide what you get
            back — and THIS is what you get back. The chapter opened on their
            objection and closes positive, on a promise. On entry the field is a wide scatter with a real
            trend: more tokens roughly buys more quality (bottom-left up to
            top-right), but the spread is enormous and it touches all four
            quadrants — cheap misses, dear misses, lucky wins, expensive wins.
            Narrate the lottery here before advancing: the correlation is real,
            yet the outcome is a gamble and much of the spend is wasted. Press
            &rarr; and the SAME attempts shift — a distribution moving, not a
            collapse, and not two archetypes. The whole cloud slides up and to
            the left: top quality now reached at a fraction of the tokens,
            spend pulled left, spread tightened. A couple still miss; that's
            honest. Attribute it to all four factors together, never to best
            practices alone — the four you just saw on the roadmap are what
            move this cloud, and each chapter from here moves it a little more.
            Each cloud carries its own fitted trend line — dashed, so it reads
            as a fit rather than as another attempt — and the pair is worth
            pointing at explicitly. Both slope the same way: more tokens
            really does buy more quality, in both worlds, so you are never
            claiming the relationship disappears.

            Narrate POSITION and SPAN, never slope. The after line is short,
            high and inside the box; the before line is long, low and ends
            outside it. Same relationship, compressed into the left quarter of
            the spend axis and lifted into ship-ready. If someone asks: the
            after line is actually slightly STEEPER, which is fine and is not
            the point — a steep short line that starts high is exactly what
            "you get there sooner" looks like.

            The lines are fitted to the body of each cloud, not to every dot:
            before's three odd attempts (the lucky win, the dear miss, the bad
            miss) and after's two remaining misses are left out of the fit but
            deliberately left on screen. If asked, say so plainly — they are
            the honest tail, and a trend line dragged around by three outliers
            would tell you less.

            Nothing else on this slide is labelled, so the two readings are
            yours to give. BEFORE: random prompts in, random results out — more
            tokens roughly buys more quality, but the spread is huge and much
            of the spend is wasted; only 3 of 18 attempts land ship-ready and
            on budget. AFTER: the same attempts, after all four factors — top
            quality at a fraction of the tokens, spend pulled left, spread
            tightened. 16 of 18. You aim, you don't gamble. Press &larr; to
            replay the scatter.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             CHAPTER 02 — "Under the hood" (wayfinder #29). Four slides:
