            but adjusted for a coding room, and the adjustment is the point.
            Work outward from the centre: the model is the engine (given — you
            pick one, you don't build it); the harness is the wrapper that
            drives it, and here it's also given — we're on a Copilot
            subscription, so don't dwell on it; skills are the reusable
            instruction packages you install on top, and they're already
            working well — no change needed, just keep building them; the
            environment is the codebase itself, and it's the one lever with
            the most headroom: the more accurate and well-structured it is,
            the better every skill performs on top of it. Note the swap out
            loud: in every primary definition — Anthropic, OpenAI, Pocock's own
            words — "agent" isn't a component, it's the NAME for these running
            together in a loop. Pocock even defines the harness as "everything
            that turns the model into an agent" — so agent is the output, not
            an ingredient. The landing line: model and harness are given; what
            you control — and what's worth the room's attention — are skills
            and the environment they read from. And phrase the compounding as
            "you build a reusable skill library on a codebase that's actually
            true," not "the agent teaches itself" — self-authoring skills is
            still aspirational (Anthropic). See docs/research for sourcing.
