                        {b.sub && <span class="sub">{b.sub}</span>}
                      </button>
                    ))}
                  </div>
                ))
              }
            </div>

            <div class="prim-panel" id="prim-panel" data-prim-panel aria-live="polite">
              <!-- Summary: what the three regions ARE. Shown on entry, and
                   returned to by the clear affordance. -->
              <article class="prim-card is-on" data-prim-card="summary">
                <span class="al-tag">The context window</span>
                <h3>Every block here is sent again, in full, every turn.</h3>
                {
                  primitiveGroups.map((g) => (
                    <p class="prim-region">
                      <b>{g.group}</b> {g.note}
                    </p>
                  ))
                }
                <p class="prim-hint">
                  Press <kbd>&rarr;</kbd> to walk the blocks, or click one.
                </p>
              </article>

              {
                primitives.map((p, i) => (
                  <article class="prim-card" data-prim-card={i}>
                    <span class="al-tag">{p.group}</span>
                    <h3>
                      {p.label}
                      {p.sub && <em> ({p.sub})</em>}
                    </h3>
                    <dl class="prim-facts">
                      <dt>Contains</dt>
                      <dd set:html={p.contains} />
                      <dt>Why it exists</dt>
                      <dd set:html={p.why} />
                      <dt>Token cost</dt>
                      <dd class="cost" set:html={p.cost} />
                    </dl>
                  </article>
                ))
              }

              <button type="button" class="prim-clear" data-prim-clear>
                &larr; clear
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Fragment spine: one invisible fragment per block, so the clicker's
               → walks them with no custom key binding and reveal's fragment
               state stays the single source of truth. Clicking a block just
               navigates to that fragment, which is why click and clicker can
               never disagree. -->
          <div class="prim-spine" data-prim-spine aria-hidden="true">
            {primitives.map(() => <span class="fragment" />)}
          </div>

          <aside class="notes">
            Open Tools first &mdash; &ldquo;paid every turn, whether you use them
            or not&rdquo; is the most surprising line here for an executive. The
            Skills block is the bridge planted early: consistent instead of a
            lottery, and paid on every turn it stays loaded. Costs here are
            deliberately qualitative &mdash; nobody publishes the size of a
            harness bundle or a skill, so any number would be invented.
          </aside>
        </section>

        <!-- 8e ─────────────────── AGENT LOOP IN ACTION (the ledger, #26 var. C) -->
        <section class="agentloop" data-section="03 — Tokenomics" data-agentloop>
          <span class="eyebrow">The loop &middot; stepped, press &rarr; to advance</span>
          <h2 class="h-set">
            <span class="h-line"><span>The whole context,</span></span>
            <span class="h-line"><span>every single turn.</span></span>
          </h2>
          <p class="al-lede">
            The agent thinks, calls a tool, reads the result, thinks again
            &mdash; re-sending the whole window each time.
          </p>

          <div class="al-stage">
            <!-- The context window itself, as a strata gutter at true
                 proportion — the harness bundle really is 68% of it. -->
            <div class="al-column" data-al-column>
              {
                loopStrata.map((b, i) => (
                  <span
                    class={`al-seg ${b.kind}`}
                    data-al-seg={i}
                    data-al-seg-step={b.step}
                    style={`--h:${b.heightPct}%`}
                    title={`${b.label} — ${b.tokens.toLocaleString("en-US")} tokens`}
                  />
                ))
              }
            </div>

            <!-- The ledger: all twelve steps stay on screen at once. -->
            <div class="al-rows">
              {
                loopRows.map((r) => (
                  <div
                    class={`al-row ${r.role}${r.billed ? " billed" : ""}`}
                    data-al-row={r.step}
                  >
                    <span class="no">{String(r.step + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                    <span class={`al-role ${r.role}`}>{r.role}</span>
                    <span class="txt" set:html={r.text} />
                    <span class="cost">{r.cost}</span>
                  </div>
                ))
              }
            </div>

            <div class="al-panel">
              <div class="al-odo">
                <span class="al-tag">running cost</span>
                <div class="big" data-al-eur>&euro;0.0000</div>
                <span class="al-tag">
                  <span data-al-aic>0.00</span> AIC &middot;
                  <span data-al-calls>0</span> model calls
                </span>
                <p class="al-ghost" data-al-ghost></p>
              </div>
              <div class="al-spark">
                <div class="al-bar-label al-window-size">
                  <span class="al-tag">context window</span>
                  <span class="al-tag al-num"><span data-al-ctx>0</span> tokens</span>
                </div>
                <div class="al-bar-row">
                  <div class="al-bar-label">
                    <span class="al-tag">input</span>
                    <span class="al-tag al-num" data-al-input>0</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="al-bar-track"><i class="al-bar-fill input" data-al-bar-input></i></div>
                </div>
                <div class="al-bar-row">
                  <div class="al-bar-label">
                    <span class="al-tag">cached</span>
                    <span class="al-tag al-num" data-al-cached>0</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="al-bar-track"><i class="al-bar-fill cached" data-al-bar-cached></i></div>
                </div>
                <div class="al-bar-row">
                  <div class="al-bar-label">
                    <span class="al-tag">output</span>
                    <span class="al-tag al-num" data-al-output>0</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="al-bar-track"><i class="al-bar-fill output" data-al-bar-output></i></div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="al-ghost">
                Gutter = the context window. Every band is re-sent on the next
                call.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="al-caveat">
            Illustrative numbers. Actual caching depends on provider and model
            cache boundaries, which change. GPT-5.4 rates, as of July 2026.
          </p>

          <!-- 12 step fragments + a 13th for the bridge. No custom binding: →
               advances, ← steps back, and re-entry from either direction
               replays correctly because reveal's fragment state is the only
               state there is — the same lesson the scatter slide records. -->
          <div class="al-spine" data-al-spine aria-hidden="true">
            {loopRows.map(() => <span class="fragment" />)}
            <span class="fragment" data-al-bridge-frag></span>
          </div>

          <p class="al-bridge" data-al-bridge>
            So how you work<br />decides what this costs.
          </p>

          <aside class="notes">
            Twelve steps, four model calls &mdash; the bars sit still while the
            agent reasons and while a tool comes back, then jump when a request
            goes out. Tool results are free until the next call re-sends them.
            Watch the cached bar pass the window three times over: nothing is
            forgotten, everything is re-sent. A cache miss re-bills the entire
            context at full input price; model-hopping mid-task is the usual
            cause, and caches expire after inactivity &mdash; 24 hours on
            OpenAI, about an hour on most others. Changing reasoning effort,
            context size or the enabled tool set mid-session kills it too. The
            routing saving is real but GitHub never quantifies it, so the only
            discounts claimed here are the two documented 10%s. This slide runs
            on GPT-5.4 while the cost dial defaults to Gemini &mdash; deliberate:
            Anthropic bills cache writes at 1.25&times; input, which would
            overstate the saving on screen here. No model was called.
          </aside>
        </section>

